---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const images = "/images/projects/state-uot-outage-forecasting";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="State-UOT Power Outage Forecasting | Amirhossein Eskorouchi"
  description="Coherent multi-horizon power outage severity forecasting with CNN/LSTM networks and state-wise unbalanced optimal transport. INFORMS 2026 Data Mining Society Data Challenge finalist, Team SIOL."
>
  <article class="page project-detail-page">
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header">
      <p class="project-detail-category">
        Time-Series Forecasting · Optimal Transport · Energy Analytics
      </p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Power Outage Forecasting with State-UOT</h1>
      <p class="project-detail-subtitle">
        Coherent multi-horizon forecasting of power outage severity with
        state-wise unbalanced optimal transport
      </p>
      <p class="project-detail-authors">
        <strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, Abhro Shome Pias,
        Dongmin Ethan Kang, and Munirul Alam · Team SIOL
      </p>
      <p class="project-detail-status award-title">
        <a href="/documents/verification/informs-2026-data-challenge-finalist.pdf"
          target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Finalist, 2026 INFORMS Data Mining Society Data Challenge
        </a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a href={`${images}/architecture.png`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the full-resolution State-UOT model diagram">
        <img src={`${images}/architecture.png`} width="1920" height="820"
          alt="A CNN/LSTM history encoder and an LSTM future-weather encoder feed a shared decoder, trained jointly with pointwise regression and state-wise unbalanced optimal transport." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The forecasting network combines observed county history and supplied
        future weather. Pointwise regression and State-UOT regularization
        jointly train both encoders and the shared decoder.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Forecasting problem</h2>
      <p>
        Power outage severity can rise abruptly and vary substantially among
        counties exposed to the same weather event. Severe observations are
        rare: just 3.0% of county-hours account for 60.5% of summed outage
        severity index (OSI) in the training data. A useful model must capture
        both the timing of these events and how severity is distributed across
        counties.
      </p>
      <p>
        The challenge provides 239 labeled training counties and 63 unseen test
        counties across Indiana, Ohio, Pennsylvania, and West Virginia. For
        each test county, the model uses 72 hours of observed weather and outage
        history, together with 144 hours of supplied future weather. Future
        outage observations are withheld. Forecasts are required at 1-, 6-,
        24-, and 48-hour horizons.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a href={`${images}/severity-concentration.png`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the training-data severity concentration charts">
        <img src={`${images}/severity-concentration.png`} width="1886" height="724"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Training-data charts show that 3.0% of county-hours carry 60.5% of summed OSI, while roughly 10% of counties account for 40 to 59% of each state's summed OSI." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Severe hours and heavily affected counties carry a disproportionate
        share of the training data's total severity.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Model and learning objective</h2>
      <p>
        A dual-stream network encodes historical weather and outage conditions
        through convolutional layers and an LSTM, while a separate LSTM encodes
        supplied future weather. A shared decoder combines the county history,
        target-hour weather representation, and normalized target-time coordinate
        to predict severity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model is trained with masked regression on log-transformed OSI and
        a state-conditioned unbalanced Sinkhorn divergence. The distributional
        term compares predicted and observed severity allocations among counties
        within each state, complementing the pointwise forecasting objective.
        Counties are represented categorically; the transport term does not use
        geographic distance.
      </p>
      <h3>Consistency across forecast horizons</h3>
      <p>
        Different forecast origins can refer to the same physical target hour.
        The decoder uses the same county-history representation and target-hour
        inputs for those requests, yielding a single consistent prediction for
        that county and target hour at inference.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Validation and final submission</h2>
      <p>
        County-level five-fold validation evaluates generalization to unseen
        counties using RMSE and MAE at all four horizons. After model selection,
        five models with different random seeds are trained on all 239 labeled
        counties. Their forecasts are averaged for the final submission.
      </p>
      <p>
        The submission contains 9,072 forecast-origin rows for the 63 test
        counties. Automated checks preserve the official row order and required
        missing targets, enforce nonnegative predictions, and verify consistency
        for repeated physical target hours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a href={`${images}/validation-design.png`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the county validation and final prediction diagram">
        <img src={`${images}/validation-design.png`} width="1894" height="790"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Five-fold county validation is separate from final training: five models are trained on all 239 training counties and averaged to forecast 63 held-out counties." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        County validation measures generalization. Final prediction uses a
        five-model ensemble trained on all available labeled counties.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Forecast patterns</h2>
      <p>
        Forecasts vary substantially across counties and target hours. In the
        final test predictions, seven counties account for 67.4% of summed
        predicted OSI, and the first 24 target hours account for 59.9%. The
        submission checks report no negative forecasts and no disagreement
        between requests for the same physical target hour.
      </p>
      <p>
        These visualizations describe model predictions for counties whose
        future outage labels are hidden; they do not measure test-set accuracy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a href={`${images}/holdout-forecasts.png`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the forecast heatmaps for all 63 test counties">
        <img src={`${images}/holdout-forecasts.png`} width="1920" height="742"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Predicted OSI heatmaps for 20 Indiana, 18 Ohio, 15 Pennsylvania, and 10 West Virginia test counties over March 14 through March 19." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Forecast severity across all 63 test counties, grouped by state. Each
        row represents a county and each column a physical target hour.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-hero-figure">
      <a href={`${images}/county-trajectories.png`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open two example county forecast trajectories">
        <img src={`${images}/county-trajectories.png`} width="1896" height="752"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Carroll County, Ohio shows predicted decline after high initial severity; Boone County, West Virginia shows later predicted escalation. Shading shows the range across five models." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Observed history and ensemble forecasts for two contrasting counties.
        Shading shows the range across the five models, rather than a calibrated
        confidence interval.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Technical stack</h2>
      <p>Python · PyTorch · GeomLoss · NumPy · Pandas · scikit-learn · Matplotlib</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <p>
        <a href="/">Return to the homepage</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
        <a href="/projects">View all projects</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
